Add GDMS recall-on-return and a per-MAC sync log (v0.124)

Dan's next three asks after confirming the GDMS assign flow works
end-to-end:

1. Recall a unit from GDMS whenever it moves back into stock - RMA
   received, an order reversed to despatch, or an order cancelled and
   returned to stock (and, later, a stock-adjustment feature not yet
   built) - and reset gdmsSyncedAt so a future re-despatch resyncs it
   fresh rather than being silently skipped as "already synced".

2. A GDMS sync log, similar to the existing Bug Reports log but with a
   search bar, date range picker and status filter - one row per MAC
   attempted (not per batch/company), showing date, source (scheduled/
   manual/manual+order number/auto+reason), order number, MAC, channel
   and status (success/failure + reason).

3. Whether to send /assign one MAC at a time instead of batches of 100,
   so one bad MAC can't hold up the rest of a batch - flagged alongside
   a concern about GDMS mistaking rapid individual calls for a DDoS.

Implemented:

- New `gdms_sync_log` table (migration V52) and `GdmsSyncLogService`,
  written exclusively by `GdmsChannelService` immediately after every
  /assign or /recycle batch call. A batch call that fails outright
  (network/auth/GDMS error, before GDMS ever looked at individual MACs)
  now writes a FAILURE row for every MAC in that batch before the
  exception is rethrown - previously such a failure left no record of
  the attempt at all.

- `GdmsChannelService.assignMacsToChannel()` now also takes the channel
  name, a MAC-to-order-number map and a free-text "source", purely so
  each log row is readable. `GdmsEndOfDayService` threads that source
  through: "Scheduled" from the 16:30 job, "Manual" from the Settings
  button, "Manual (Order BNS-1234)" from the per-order button (looked
  up from the order's own despatch movements).

- New `GdmsChannelService.reclaimMacs()`, calling GDMS's /channel/recycle
  (confirmed live via the doc's own api_data.js: request field is
  "macs", not "macList" like /assign - a genuinely different field name,
  not a typo carried over - same batching/per-MAC-logging shape).

- New `GdmsRecallService`, shared by `OrderReversalService`
  (reverseToDespatch/cancelAndReturnToStock) and `RmaService#receive()`.
  Collects every already-synced StockItem being returned, reclaims all
  of them from GDMS in one batched call, and always resets
  gdmsSyncedAt afterwards regardless of whether the GDMS call itself
  succeeded - a recall failure must never block the underlying RMA/
  reversal/cancellation, since the stock is physically back in the
  warehouse either way. The failed/skipped attempt is still visible on
  the new log page even though it didn't stop anything.

- New GDMS Log page (Admin nav), mirroring PaymentTracking.tsx's shape:
  search input, status/operation filters, DateRangePicker, everything
  filtered client-side against a plain unfiltered GET - the same
  pattern every other log/history page in this codebase already uses,
  confirmed via research there's no exception to deviate from.

Decided against switching /assign to one-MAC-per-call: GDMS's own
errorMacList already isolates individually-rejected MACs within an
otherwise-successful batch (confirmed from the doc's own example
response), so today's design already stops one bad MAC from blocking
the rest of its batch. Going to one-per-call would multiply the number
of signed API calls roughly a hundredfold for no real benefit, and
directly increases the rate-limiting/DDoS-detection risk that was the
actual worry behind the question - kept batches of 100.

gdms-api-findings.md (project doc) to be updated with the /recycle
field-name finding.

